<br>
Our research develops <strong>cyber-physical manufacturing and security frameworks</strong> that bridge physical manufacturing processes with digital information to enable secure, traceable, and intelligent manufacturing systems. A key focus is the development of <strong>Physical Unclonable Functions (PUFs)</strong> that exploit process-induced randomness and unique micro-/nanoscale surface features as physical fingerprints for part authentication and identification. By integrating advanced manufacturing, imaging, feature extraction, data-driven methods, and digital authentication, we create <strong>physical–digital security architectures</strong> for part traceability, tamper detection, and counterfeit prevention throughout the manufacturing lifecycle. <br>

<b>Our Current Research Directions:</b>
<ul>
  <li><b>Process–signature correlations for secure part identification and traceability</b></li>
  <li><b>Algorithmic and AI-driven authentication and verification frameworks</b></li>
  <li><b>Multimodal PUFs integrating optical, topographical, and electrical signatures</b></li>
  <li><b>Cybersecurity and blockchain integration for secure manufacturing and digital traceability</b></li>
</ul>

